## 3. Methodology

### Research Design

The study uses a **sequential mixed-methods** design:

| Phase | Design | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Phase 1 | Quantitative + qualitative survey | Online preliminary survey (pilot completed, n = 42) on trust, fatigue, explanation preferences |
| Phase 2 | Quantitative controlled experiment | Between-subjects: Condition A (AI-only alerts) vs Condition B (AI+XAI alerts) |
| Phase 3 | Optional qualitative interviews | Short interviews (n ≈ 5) to explain trust/distrust reasons |

Overall paradigm: **pragmatic** (measurable performance + perception/insight).

### Participant Recruitment

Participants will be recruited using **purposive and convenience sampling** through:

- university / SIHE student communication channels  
- class groups and eligible student communities  
- voluntary sharing with people who have IT, networking, cybersecurity or SOC-related study/practice interest  

Recruitment messages will include a short information statement (purpose, time required, voluntary nature, anonymity). No pressure or coercion will be used.

**Eligibility:** 18 years or older; relevant interest or background in IT/cybersecurity preferred.

### Sample Size

| Phase | Planned / achieved sample |
| --- | --- |
| Phase 1 survey | Pilot completed **n = 42**; target range approximately 40–100 for broader collection if continued |
| Phase 2 experiment | Approximately **40–60** participants randomly assigned to Condition A or B |
| Phase 3 interviews | Approximately **5** volunteers from consenting participants |

### Data Collection Methods

1. **Online questionnaire** (Google Forms / Microsoft Forms): demographics, Likert items, explanation preference, one open-ended comment, consent confirmation.  
2. **Controlled triage task** (simulated alerts only): participants review 8–10 alerts under AI-only or AI+XAI presentation; decisions and confidence recorded.  
3. **Optional brief interview:** open questions about trust in AI alerts (no recording of sensitive organisational secrets).

**Important:** No live victim data, no real network compromise, and no actionable exploit instructions will be used.

### Data Analysis Plan

- Survey: frequencies, percentages, means/SD, preference rankings, cross-tabs (e.g., experience × trust), thematic coding of open responses.  
- Experiment: descriptive statistics; independent-samples t-tests or Mann–Whitney U for H1–H2; ANOVA/regression for H3; effect sizes (Cohen’s d); thematic analysis of comments.  
- Software: Excel and/or R.

