## 4. Ethical Considerations

### 4.1 Voluntary and Informed Participation

**How will you provide clear, accessible information to participants?**  
Before starting, participants will see a plain-language information statement covering: project title and purpose; what they will do; approximate time (survey ~8–10 minutes; experiment session separately explained); voluntary participation; anonymity/confidentiality; how data will be used (academic MIT653 purposes only); and contact details for the student researcher / supervisor if needed.

**How will you obtain and document informed consent?**  
Consent will be obtained electronically via a required consent item (e.g., “I have read the information and agree to take part anonymously — Yes”) before survey/experiment items. Interview consent will be confirmed again before any optional interview begins. Consent wording is also included in the proposal appendix.

**How will you ensure participants can withdraw freely at any stage?**  
Participants may close the form or stop the session at any time without penalty. For anonymous online survey responses already submitted, withdrawal of an individual record may not be possible if no identifier exists; this will be stated in the information sheet. For the experiment/interviews, participants may withdraw before or during participation without negative consequences.

### 4.2 Minimising Harm and Discomfort

**Potential risks**

| Risk type | Assessment |
| --- | --- |
| Psychological | Low — possible mild frustration with alerts or reflection on workplace alert fatigue |
| Physical | None expected |
| Legal | Low — simulated content only; no illegal activity requested |
| Cultural / social | Low — questions avoid sensitive personal identity topics beyond role/experience |
| Dual-use / security | Managed — no real exploits, no live systems, no confidential organisational logs |

**Steps to reduce or manage risks**

- Use **simulated** security alerts only.  
- Keep participation short and voluntary.  
- Allow participants to skip uncomfortable open questions.  
- Exclude actionable attack instructions.  
- Debrief after the experiment explaining that alerts were simulated.  
- Classify overall risk level as **Low**.

### 4.3 Protecting Confidentiality and Privacy
**Contribution by: Regina Basnet**

Although the overall research risk is considered low, a clear procedure will be followed if a participant experiences stress, discomfort or concern during the survey, experiment or optional interview.

If a participant shows or reports discomfort:

1. The research activity will be paused or stopped immediately.
2. The participant will be reminded that participation is voluntary and that they may withdraw without penalty.
3. The participant will not be required to explain or justify their decision to stop.
4. No further research activity will continue unless the participant freely chooses to proceed.
5. The participant will receive an appropriate debrief explaining the purpose of the simulated cybersecurity scenarios.
6. Where appropriate, relevant support or contact information will be provided.
7. Any significant or unexpected ethical issue will be documented and reported to the project supervisor, Dr. Sudath Heiyanthuduwage.

To further minimise psychological discomfort, participants will not be individually ranked or publicly compared based on their cybersecurity performance. Results will be analysed and reported in aggregate or de-identified form.

This procedure ensures that participant wellbeing takes priority over completion of the research activity.

**How will you store, protect, and limit access to personal data?**  
Data will be stored on password-protected accounts (institutional / authorised student accounts). Access limited to the research group members for academic analysis only. No unnecessary personal identifiers (full legal name, student number, phone, employer secrets) will be requested for analysis.

**Will the study be anonymous or de-identified?**  
The online survey is designed to be **anonymous** (no names required for analysis). Experiment/interview notes will be **de-identified** (Participant codes such as P01) if any contact is needed for scheduling. Results will be reported in aggregate form.

### 4.4 Ensuring Fair and Respectful Treatment
#### Privacy and Data Minimisation

**Contribution by: Regina Basnet**

The research team will apply a data-minimisation approach by collecting only information necessary to answer the research questions.

The anonymous online survey will not require participants' names for analysis. Where participants need to be distinguished during experimental or interview activities, de-identified participant codes such as P01, P02 and P03 will be used instead of names.

Demographic information will be limited to research-relevant variables such as participant role and level of IT/cybersecurity experience. Participants will not be asked to disclose passwords, confidential workplace information, private organisational security incidents or other unnecessary sensitive information.

When qualitative comments are reported, potentially identifying details will be removed or generalised before inclusion in the final research report.

These measures reduce the amount of personal information collected and minimise the possibility of identifying individual participants.

**Inclusivity, fairness, and cultural sensitivity**  
Recruitment will be open to eligible adult volunteers with relevant interest; wording will be respectful and non-discriminatory. No participant will be excluded on the basis of gender, ethnicity, or cultural background.

**Preventing bias or unequal treatment**  
All participants in the same phase receive the same information and procedures. Experiment assignment to Condition A/B will be random. No preferential grading or academic benefit beyond voluntary learning experience is offered for participation (unless a small voucher budget is used equally under the approved proposal budget).

### 4.5 Honesty with Professional Colleagues

**Transparent reporting**  
Findings will be reported honestly, including limitations (convenience sample; simulated alerts; proposal-stage experiment not yet fully executed). Results will not be fabricated, selectively deleted, or misrepresented.

**Acknowledging contributions**  
Group members, supervisor (Dr. Sudath Heiyanthuduwage), and participants (in aggregate) will be acknowledged. Sources will be cited. AI assistance for language/formatting, if used, will be declared according to SIHE academic integrity / AI rules.

